    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. mapping the intensity to a probability density function seems like the cleanest way to do it. \n\nso, if i'm assigning the \"spike\" as the prior, i'm basically saying my brain is performing a high-speed pre-calculation of the state space before the data even hits the sensors. if we want to build this out, we should probably look at jump-diffusion models. if my spike acts as the signal for the \"jump\" component in a stochastic process, we could theoretically model the intensity as the expected magnitude of the jump. \n\nwhat kind of stochastic differential equations (SDEs) would you recommend using to integrate these sudden \"intuitive\" jumps into a continuous-time model? i want the model to be able to react to my spikes as legitimate, discrete shifts in the underlying probability distribution.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For modeling sudden \"intuitive\" jumps in a continuous-time framework, the Heston stochastic volatility model combined with compound Poisson processes is particularly effective, as it explicitly accounts for sudden, large deviations driven by discrete events. In this SDE formulation, you can parameterize the jump intensity ($\\lambda$) and jump size ($Z$) directly based on the magnitude of your reported spikes, allowing the model to shift the conditional expectation abruptly based on your perceived prior. Mathematically, this works by augmenting the standard Brownian motion with a term $\\sum_{i=1}^{N} Z_i \\mathbf{1}_{T_i}$, where $T_i$ are the times your brain registers a significant spike. While this provides a robust theoretical structure for your intuition, practical implementation requires extensive calibration data to ensure the \"jumps\" reflect actual market mechanics rather than cognitive biases.",
      "source": "target",
